1
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:14,041
<i>Do you really think</i>
<i>you could get this far by yourself?</i>

2
00:00:29,958 --> 00:00:32,416
No one in the world besides me

3
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,250
can cross that line and come in here.

4
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:39,541
Do you know what would happen
if this machine were not scanning my iris?

5
00:00:40,416 --> 00:00:43,083
I suppose the response would be dramatic.

6
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:49,333
Correct.

7
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:55,333
If it hadn't correctly detected my iris
within ten seconds,

8
00:00:55,416 --> 00:00:59,333
methane gas would pour in
through all those nozzles,

9
00:00:59,416 --> 00:01:00,666
and then…

10
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:02,541
A spark.

11
00:01:02,625 --> 00:01:06,375
Exactly. All this
would become a great ring of fire.

12
00:01:06,458 --> 00:01:11,000
Like the third ring
in the seventh circle of Dante's hell.

13
00:01:11,083 --> 00:01:15,041
So, either a Marvel character shows up,

14
00:01:15,125 --> 00:01:17,958
a fireproof one with my eyes,

15
00:01:18,041 --> 00:01:19,875
or no one…

16
00:01:20,375 --> 00:01:24,708
No one could get to this door.

17
00:01:26,375 --> 00:01:27,791
Look at this masterpiece.

18
00:01:27,875 --> 00:01:29,291
No handles,

19
00:01:29,375 --> 00:01:31,208
no locks,

20
00:01:31,708 --> 00:01:33,375
no hydraulic keys.

21
00:01:33,958 --> 00:01:36,250
Mysteriously shut.

22
00:01:36,750 --> 00:01:38,208
What do you think?

23
00:01:43,833 --> 00:01:45,041
Brilliant.

24
00:01:57,458 --> 00:01:58,500
Keila!

25
00:01:59,625 --> 00:02:01,125
Keila, are you there?

26
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:02,291
Keila!

27
00:02:03,458 --> 00:02:04,750
What?

28
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:06,458
I was right.

29
00:02:06,541 --> 00:02:09,041
- I was right!
- About what, Damián?

30
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:12,791
He has a barbecue down there.
It's a trap for roasting elephants.

31
00:02:12,875 --> 00:02:16,000
Getting in there is completely impossible.

32
00:02:16,083 --> 00:02:17,083
He's a genius.

33
00:02:17,166 --> 00:02:18,875
A fricking master, he's…

34
00:02:19,583 --> 00:02:21,750
Excuse me. The wine.

35
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,333
- Damián.
- Everything's stained now.

36
00:02:24,916 --> 00:02:26,166
It's okay.

37
00:02:42,208 --> 00:02:44,125
The painting will leave Kraków

38
00:02:44,208 --> 00:02:47,708
in a suitcase at 8:15 a.m.
on a normal flight.

39
00:02:47,791 --> 00:02:50,583
It'll be protected with wood,
bulletproof glass, a seal.

40
00:02:50,666 --> 00:02:52,583
Like all the artwork loaned by the…

41
00:02:52,666 --> 00:02:54,083
Thank you. …Czartoryski.

42
00:02:54,166 --> 00:02:56,875
We'll come back to this later.
Time of arrival.

43
00:02:56,958 --> 00:02:58,625
Time…

44
00:02:58,708 --> 00:03:00,791
12:30 p.m. at Seville's airport.

45
00:03:00,875 --> 00:03:03,916
There it'll be picked up
by a private security company,

46
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,750
specialized in transporting art.

47
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:07,666
I got it here.

48
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:11,041
And, like any truck transporting
a treasure, it'll be armored.

49
00:03:11,125 --> 00:03:14,541
The company is called Blindesart,
and these are their trucks.

50
00:03:15,125 --> 00:03:19,625
A veritable moving fortress,
and here begin our problems.

51
00:03:19,708 --> 00:03:22,291
No, there we ambush the truck.

52
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:25,125
And shoot the glass point-blank like this.

53
00:03:27,166 --> 00:03:30,541
We get the guards out,
and in 12 seconds, you have the box open.

54
00:03:30,625 --> 00:03:34,375
Very good idea,
but there are some issues to bear in mind.

55
00:03:34,458 --> 00:03:37,208
First, the glass is bulletproof.

56
00:03:37,291 --> 00:03:43,666
Second, unlike other armored money trucks,
the guards in this one don't have keys.

57
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:45,583
- They don't?
- No. There is no key.

58
00:03:45,666 --> 00:03:47,166
They have a combination,

59
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:49,916
and the guards don't know it, for safety.

60
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,416
And if we use the moving truck technique?

61
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,041
- Which one's that?
- You don't know it?

62
00:03:56,833 --> 00:03:59,041
- From <i>Fast & Furious.</i>
<i>- Fast & Furious?</i>

63
00:03:59,125 --> 00:04:02,083
Yes, kidnap a truck,
and stick it into a bigger truck.

64
00:04:02,166 --> 00:04:05,541
Good idea,
but we'd have to be behind the wheel.

65
00:04:05,625 --> 00:04:07,625
Well, don't you get picky now.

66
00:04:07,708 --> 00:04:10,041
After all, it's a fake plan
to fool the duke.

67
00:04:10,125 --> 00:04:13,958
No, Candela.
The plan needs to be perfect and real

68
00:04:14,041 --> 00:04:15,666
to shake that smug fool.

69
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:19,458
And, the greater the difficulties,
the more brilliant the plan.

70
00:04:19,541 --> 00:04:20,833
Damn.

71
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:23,916
You talk a pretty game. You're so smart.

72
00:04:24,791 --> 00:04:27,750
I'll end up feeling sad
we're not stealing that lady.

73
00:04:27,833 --> 00:04:29,958
- Why?
- Because it irritates me.

74
00:04:30,041 --> 00:04:33,333
Our first robbery together
as a couple, and it's fake.

75
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,291
And the plan is going to be so pretty.

76
00:04:39,458 --> 00:04:42,666
Hey, look. Thieves on duty again, huh?

77
00:04:43,166 --> 00:04:45,833
I'm going to Pepe's tavern for some eggs.

78
00:04:45,916 --> 00:04:48,916
I'm starving.
You have your chit-chat with the band.

79
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:50,083
Bye.

80
00:04:54,666 --> 00:04:56,291
- Hello.
<i>- I couldn't call before.</i>

81
00:04:56,375 --> 00:04:58,458
You won't believe what happened.

82
00:04:58,541 --> 00:05:01,458
We left port escorted by the marine guard.

83
00:05:07,208 --> 00:05:11,583
<i>As the boat was about to leave, diplomats</i>
<i>and important people started to arrive</i>

84
00:05:13,958 --> 00:05:16,916
<i>to give their respects</i>
<i>to the illustrious passengers.</i>

85
00:05:19,375 --> 00:05:20,708
You know what that means.

86
00:05:20,791 --> 00:05:24,125
No one's searching that pallet.

87
00:05:24,208 --> 00:05:25,083
Precisely.

88
00:05:25,166 --> 00:05:29,166
Those passengers
are the perfect alibi to avoid suspicion.

89
00:05:29,250 --> 00:05:33,750
<i>- Have you found the rice?</i>
- I searched everywhere, but no luck.

90
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:35,083
<i>Not a trace.</i>

91
00:05:35,166 --> 00:05:39,500
<i>There must be some kind of secret hold.</i>
<i>A hidden area.</i>

92
00:05:39,583 --> 00:05:41,416
They're stowing the goods somewhere.

93
00:05:42,583 --> 00:05:44,458
<i>I measured the height of two levels.</i>

94
00:05:44,958 --> 00:05:48,208
<i>That of the holds and engine room</i>
<i>and the cabins level.</i>

95
00:05:48,791 --> 00:05:51,708
<i>And it turns out</i>
<i>there's a 65-cm gap between them.</i>

96
00:05:51,791 --> 00:05:53,541
<i>You know what that means?</i>

97
00:05:53,625 --> 00:05:56,875
<i>That there's a hidden cavity</i>
<i>between those levels.</i>

98
00:05:56,958 --> 00:05:58,083
I'll look into it.

99
00:05:58,166 --> 00:06:00,625
It must be a technical space for pipes.

100
00:06:00,708 --> 00:06:02,875
For AC or something like that.

101
00:06:02,958 --> 00:06:06,041
It could also be a false floor
to hide contraband.

102
00:06:06,125 --> 00:06:07,666
<i>Only one way to find out.</i>

103
00:06:07,750 --> 00:06:10,750
You're not thinking
of making a hole in your cabin.

104
00:06:10,833 --> 00:06:12,458
<i>- Right?</i>
- Why not?

105
00:06:12,541 --> 00:06:13,625
<i>Because!</i>

106
00:06:14,833 --> 00:06:16,541
That's your problem in life.

107
00:06:17,625 --> 00:06:19,833
You do the opposite
of what you have to do.

108
00:06:20,541 --> 00:06:24,500
<i>When people ask you to go, you don't.</i>
<i>When they tell you to stay, you run away.</i>

109
00:06:24,583 --> 00:06:28,833
When you're unfaithful to your lover,
you humiliate him and blame him.

110
00:06:29,708 --> 00:06:31,083
<i>You're your worst enemy.</i>

111
00:06:31,166 --> 00:06:32,333
That's your opinion.

112
00:06:32,416 --> 00:06:36,875
I only hope you realize I'm right
before it's too late.

113
00:06:36,958 --> 00:06:40,083
You'll crawl into a hole
in the middle of the ocean.

114
00:06:40,166 --> 00:06:41,583
With no escape route.

115
00:06:41,666 --> 00:06:45,125
You're assuming I'll be caught.
What if I make it?

116
00:06:45,208 --> 00:06:47,916
Don't do it! That's an order!

117
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:49,500
Fine.

118
00:07:56,708 --> 00:07:59,166
<i>I love swans.</i>

119
00:08:10,041 --> 00:08:13,458
<i>I can't stop smiling</i>
<i>since I saw you in your white shirt.</i>

120
00:08:16,750 --> 00:08:18,750
<i>Dinner is at 8:00, on your boat.</i>

121
00:08:20,041 --> 00:08:23,041
<i>I haven't been on a date since I was 15.</i>

122
00:08:24,625 --> 00:08:27,250
<i>Just the thought</i>
<i>sends shivers down my spine.</i>

123
00:08:27,333 --> 00:08:29,041
<i>I'm so flustered…</i>

124
00:08:29,625 --> 00:08:31,208
<i>I'll lose my babysitter.</i>

125
00:08:33,583 --> 00:08:37,666
<i>Please, don't think me a duck.</i>
<i>Like you, I have the soul of a swan.</i>

126
00:08:38,166 --> 00:08:40,458
<i>The danger is exciting.</i>

127
00:08:42,208 --> 00:08:43,625
Yes, what is it?

128
00:08:43,708 --> 00:08:44,916
Damián, it's Roi.

129
00:08:47,791 --> 00:08:49,416
Get out.

130
00:08:51,083 --> 00:08:52,500
Get out!

131
00:09:03,291 --> 00:09:06,250
- What are you looking at?
- I saw your books.

132
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:08,000
What books?

133
00:09:08,083 --> 00:09:11,250
The ones on divination and palm reading.

134
00:09:11,333 --> 00:09:12,750
Oh, that?

135
00:09:12,833 --> 00:09:15,333
Yeah. My grandma used to read palms.

136
00:09:16,041 --> 00:09:18,583
So did my mother, and so do I.

137
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,416
No. I only read for people I don't know.

138
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,666
No.

139
00:09:26,750 --> 00:09:27,916
Come on.

140
00:09:28,458 --> 00:09:30,166
I'm asking as a personal favor.

141
00:09:30,833 --> 00:09:34,875
I could use a look into the future
at this moment in my life, please.

142
00:09:36,041 --> 00:09:37,166
Please.

143
00:09:45,458 --> 00:09:47,083
Don't get nervous.

144
00:09:48,875 --> 00:09:51,750
Hey, look. You have the line of success.

145
00:09:51,833 --> 00:09:53,583
Not many people have it, mind you.

146
00:09:54,083 --> 00:09:56,541
Only those who are lucky.

147
00:09:57,041 --> 00:09:58,416
Yours is very pronounced.

148
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:01,333
Look, there it is.

149
00:10:01,916 --> 00:10:03,125
Right.

150
00:10:04,416 --> 00:10:05,750
- And love?
- Oh.

151
00:10:05,833 --> 00:10:07,291
Love?

152
00:10:08,833 --> 00:10:11,583
Your love line is very braided.

153
00:10:11,666 --> 00:10:12,916
What does that mean?

154
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,416
That you have many, many stories,
and very intense.

155
00:10:19,416 --> 00:10:21,916
That's the line of someone
who loves for real.

156
00:10:23,083 --> 00:10:25,458
But here things get…

157
00:10:25,541 --> 00:10:28,208
Look, here it's very intense and red.

158
00:10:28,291 --> 00:10:30,666
This is a great love.

159
00:10:31,416 --> 00:10:33,208
Andalusian, I'd say.

160
00:10:33,291 --> 00:10:35,125
A very good-looking girl.

161
00:10:36,041 --> 00:10:39,458
A bit of a thief
and an occasional pyromaniac.

162
00:10:39,541 --> 00:10:41,750
Something nice
is going to happen with her.

163
00:10:44,166 --> 00:10:45,291
What?

164
00:10:50,583 --> 00:10:54,500
I only read the future.
I don't dictate it.

165
00:10:55,083 --> 00:10:56,375
But it's very beautiful.

166
00:10:57,375 --> 00:10:59,000
Will I have to wait long?

167
00:11:00,083 --> 00:11:01,291
Are you in a hurry?

168
00:11:01,875 --> 00:11:04,416
More than a hurry. I'm dying to live it.

169
00:11:10,708 --> 00:11:12,125
Tell me everything.

170
00:11:20,750 --> 00:11:22,875
Well, I have good news and bad news.

171
00:11:23,375 --> 00:11:24,791
Shall we start with the bad?

172
00:11:27,125 --> 00:11:28,916
The bad news is

173
00:11:29,416 --> 00:11:31,333
you'll get seriously ill.

174
00:11:37,541 --> 00:11:38,750
And the good news?

175
00:11:41,833 --> 00:11:43,958
That you won't die from that.

176
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
Thieves on duty.

177
00:11:58,416 --> 00:12:01,750
Take your band's calls. I'm off
to pick watermelons with my father.

178
00:12:05,958 --> 00:12:08,458
<i>- Hello.</i>
- All four of us are on speakerphone.

179
00:12:08,541 --> 00:12:10,916
- Can you talk?
<i>- Go ahead.</i>

180
00:12:11,666 --> 00:12:15,000
Down there, we have an igloo-shaped
titanium armored vault

181
00:12:15,083 --> 00:12:16,916
in a hallway that needs to ID

182
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,083
the duke's iris in 10 seconds
or it turns into a ring of fire.

183
00:12:20,166 --> 00:12:22,958
The hallway houses deposits
of flammable methane gas.

184
00:12:23,041 --> 00:12:23,875
Exotic.

185
00:12:23,958 --> 00:12:27,458
Did you work out all that megalomaniac
macabre fantasy with the GPR?

186
00:12:28,041 --> 00:12:29,833
No. I saw it with my own eyes.

187
00:12:29,916 --> 00:12:32,041
Last night, strolling around the palace,

188
00:12:32,125 --> 00:12:34,416
I found the duke,
who couldn't sleep either.

189
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:37,041
We had some wine,
went down to the stables…

190
00:12:37,125 --> 00:12:38,541
The stables?

191
00:12:39,750 --> 00:12:40,833
Late at night?

192
00:12:40,916 --> 00:12:42,375
Yes, what are you thinking?

193
00:12:42,458 --> 00:12:47,583
Nothing. But you just kicked me
out of your room screaming, on all fours.

194
00:12:47,666 --> 00:12:49,416
It seemed like you weren't alone.

195
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:53,208
You said he took you down to the stables,
and I connected the dots.

196
00:12:53,291 --> 00:12:56,875
What about the bodyguard?
I see a weird relationship there.

197
00:12:56,958 --> 00:12:59,291
Maybe he's also taken
for a roll in the hay.

198
00:12:59,375 --> 00:13:01,291
To the stables, not the hay.

199
00:13:01,375 --> 00:13:03,458
<i>It was a night of male bonding.</i>

200
00:13:03,541 --> 00:13:04,958
Of friendship.

201
00:13:05,041 --> 00:13:07,333
We hugged a horse.

202
00:13:07,416 --> 00:13:10,208
It's his system to reduce stress levels.

203
00:13:10,291 --> 00:13:14,208
Then we talked about life.
We sang a Mexican corrido.

204
00:13:14,291 --> 00:13:15,875
Great. And then?

205
00:13:15,958 --> 00:13:18,458
He took me to the crypt.
He showed me everything.

206
00:13:18,541 --> 00:13:21,916
The first door is opened
with an alphanumeric code, as we thought,

207
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,500
but as soon as you're
in the circular hallway,

208
00:13:24,583 --> 00:13:27,500
if the system does not ID the duke's iris…

209
00:13:28,458 --> 00:13:29,458
you're torched.

210
00:13:31,875 --> 00:13:34,791
<i>It's an inviolable masterwork.</i>

211
00:13:34,875 --> 00:13:37,875
The man is a genius.
We won't be able to steal his money.

212
00:13:37,958 --> 00:13:40,041
Of course we will.

213
00:13:40,125 --> 00:13:41,833
<i>There's always a way.</i>

214
00:13:41,916 --> 00:13:43,541
Oh, yeah? What?

215
00:13:43,625 --> 00:13:46,083
Take his eyeballs so we can scan them?

216
00:13:46,166 --> 00:13:49,041
Maybe we won't have
to gouge anyone's eyeballs out.

217
00:13:49,125 --> 00:13:52,583
We have enough data. We can
make calculations and find other ways.

218
00:13:52,666 --> 00:13:53,958
Exactly.

219
00:13:54,041 --> 00:13:56,541
Are you thinking what I'm thinking?
The oxygen.

220
00:13:56,625 --> 00:13:59,000
It's a hypothesis,
but with the data we have,

221
00:13:59,083 --> 00:14:02,791
we can calculate the volume of oxygen
in the hallway and combustion time.

222
00:14:03,791 --> 00:14:08,458
And could you now give us
the version for the general public?

223
00:14:08,541 --> 00:14:12,791
Once the fire's consumed all the oxygen
in the hallway, it will die out.

224
00:14:12,875 --> 00:14:14,916
<i>Then we'll move to the next phase.</i>

225
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Opening the igloo's door.

226
00:14:17,083 --> 00:14:18,833
Without oxygen in the air?

227
00:14:19,416 --> 00:14:20,833
We can take oxygen tanks.

228
00:14:20,916 --> 00:14:23,125
And fireproof suits to withstand the fire.

229
00:14:23,208 --> 00:14:26,625
For the love of God, those only work
for a short period of time.

230
00:14:26,708 --> 00:14:27,541
<i>Mere seconds.</i>

231
00:14:27,625 --> 00:14:29,625
Any miscalculation could be fatal.

232
00:14:29,708 --> 00:14:31,125
We'll round up for safety.

233
00:14:31,208 --> 00:14:33,541
And how will you open the titanium door?

234
00:14:33,625 --> 00:14:36,666
Without oxygen,
you can't ignite a thermal lance,

235
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:37,833
<i>so what will you do?</i>

236
00:14:37,916 --> 00:14:40,416
What's gotten into you, Damián?

237
00:14:42,583 --> 00:14:43,833
You don't want to do it?

238
00:14:43,916 --> 00:14:46,916
It's not that I don't want to.
I'm assessing the risk.

239
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:48,958
Don't start lying to me now.

240
00:14:50,416 --> 00:14:53,333
You're involved like a schoolboy
about to bed the duchess.

241
00:14:53,416 --> 00:14:56,000
<i>With messages all day</i>
<i>and clandestine rendezvous,</i>

242
00:14:56,083 --> 00:14:58,750
<i>while you become</i>
<i>her husband's best friend.</i>

243
00:14:59,625 --> 00:15:00,833
I know you.

244
00:15:01,583 --> 00:15:03,500
You're beginning to feel empathy.

245
00:15:04,083 --> 00:15:05,833
<i>Maybe even thinking</i>

246
00:15:05,916 --> 00:15:08,375
this robbery is a betrayal, am I wrong?

247
00:15:09,541 --> 00:15:10,833
<i>What was your motto?</i>

248
00:15:12,416 --> 00:15:15,291
"A heist is surgery and cold heads."

249
00:15:15,375 --> 00:15:18,500
<i>Do you think</i>
<i>you're behaving like a professional?</i>

250
00:15:18,583 --> 00:15:19,583
I guess not.

251
00:15:19,666 --> 00:15:23,500
Whatever must be done down there,
we'll solve with science.

252
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:25,250
As we've always done.

253
00:15:32,416 --> 00:15:33,791
Check, please.

254
00:15:37,333 --> 00:15:38,875
This should cover it.

255
00:15:49,583 --> 00:15:51,500
- Camille.
- Hi.

256
00:15:54,291 --> 00:15:56,041
I see you still remember my name.

257
00:15:57,541 --> 00:15:59,291
I haven't forgotten yours.

258
00:16:00,333 --> 00:16:02,375
I have a surprise for you at my hotel.

259
00:16:02,916 --> 00:16:04,541
- Come with me.
- No.

260
00:16:04,625 --> 00:16:07,250
I can't go right now.
Why don't we talk here?

261
00:16:08,458 --> 00:16:09,875
It's not an invitation.

262
00:16:13,333 --> 00:16:14,625
It's an order.

263
00:16:16,583 --> 00:16:17,583
Shall we?

264
00:16:19,166 --> 00:16:23,416
I calculated the oxygen volume
in the vault's hallway.

265
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:26,041
It's 79.68 cubic meters.

266
00:16:26,125 --> 00:16:29,833
Estimating a methane inflow
of 0.2 cubic meters per second,

267
00:16:29,916 --> 00:16:31,875
through the nozzles,

268
00:16:31,958 --> 00:16:35,416
the ball of fire shouldn't take
more than 45 seconds

269
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:38,000
to consume all the oxygen in there.

270
00:16:38,083 --> 00:16:40,250
That's the time the suit needs to resist?

271
00:16:40,333 --> 00:16:41,166
That's right.

272
00:16:41,250 --> 00:16:43,416
At ignition
and for some tenths of a second,

273
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:47,416
combustion temperature will reach 1,963ºC.

274
00:16:47,500 --> 00:16:50,208
Then it will stabilize
between 400 and 600 degrees.

275
00:16:50,291 --> 00:16:53,166
There are materials
that resist those temperatures.

276
00:16:53,250 --> 00:16:54,916
Fibers like Nomex,

277
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,500
or aluminized suits
like firefighters wear.

278
00:16:57,583 --> 00:17:00,041
How will we know if the suit works?

279
00:17:00,125 --> 00:17:01,333
We can test it.

280
00:17:01,416 --> 00:17:02,666
How?

281
00:17:02,750 --> 00:17:04,458
With a flamethrower.

282
00:17:04,541 --> 00:17:06,583
You think this is a escape room, Roi?

283
00:17:06,666 --> 00:17:09,500
First demolition, now what? Conflagration?

284
00:17:09,583 --> 00:17:11,375
It's the adrenaline boost I need.

285
00:17:12,250 --> 00:17:13,250
I'm not scared.

286
00:17:13,333 --> 00:17:16,958
We need someone with a cool head
in there, not a kamikaze.

287
00:17:17,041 --> 00:17:18,291
- Bruce will go.
- What?

288
00:17:18,375 --> 00:17:20,416
You have a volunteer and pick me?

289
00:17:21,791 --> 00:17:23,416
I don't want to go.

290
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:25,250
It's technically possible.

291
00:17:25,333 --> 00:17:27,666
We'll be at 600 degrees in that hole.

292
00:17:27,750 --> 00:17:28,875
I'm not barbecue meat.

293
00:17:28,958 --> 00:17:31,041
I'm not arguing. You'll both go.

294
00:17:31,625 --> 00:17:34,208
Figure out where we can source
the fireproof suits.

295
00:17:34,291 --> 00:17:36,041
And scram. I have things to do.

296
00:17:52,375 --> 00:17:53,541
It's here.

297
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:57,666
You're as beautiful as always.

298
00:17:59,250 --> 00:18:01,500
Although you seem different.
Must be the gun.

299
00:18:01,583 --> 00:18:03,125
Do you see me as different?

300
00:18:03,625 --> 00:18:04,625
Really?

301
00:18:08,791 --> 00:18:09,791
Maybe.

302
00:18:18,791 --> 00:18:20,708
I went through some life changes.

303
00:18:21,916 --> 00:18:26,166
An ex-husband with a secret child
ended up in jail.

304
00:18:26,750 --> 00:18:30,083
And a slippery lover
stood me up in Singapore.

305
00:18:34,458 --> 00:18:35,750
Why didn't you go?

306
00:18:36,250 --> 00:18:41,916
I got stuck in Colombia's Coffee Axis
when a volcano erupted.

307
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,125
They cancelled all flights.

308
00:18:45,250 --> 00:18:46,625
What are you doing?

309
00:18:48,041 --> 00:18:49,166
Easy.

310
00:18:49,250 --> 00:18:53,583
All the country's roads
were jammed for days.

311
00:18:53,666 --> 00:18:55,666
It was impossible to get out.

312
00:18:56,166 --> 00:19:01,041
So, desperate,
I called the Blue Moon in Singapore.

313
00:19:01,125 --> 00:19:04,250
I was hopeful again,
because a waiter called Ralph

314
00:19:04,333 --> 00:19:05,791
agreed to help me.

315
00:19:05,875 --> 00:19:07,291
Help you how?

316
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:10,625
<i>I bought a ticket to Argentina.</i>

317
00:19:10,708 --> 00:19:14,791
<i>I asked him to print it and put it</i>
<i>in a red envelope with my phone number,</i>

318
00:19:14,875 --> 00:19:18,125
<i>and to write your name on it</i>
<i>and give it to you.</i>

319
00:19:21,041 --> 00:19:24,666
I thought it romantic to meet
in another continent because of a volcano

320
00:19:24,750 --> 00:19:26,416
because, after all,

321
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:28,958
our relationship was like a volcano.

322
00:19:30,375 --> 00:19:31,833
I never got that envelope.

323
00:19:32,875 --> 00:19:37,833
I probably misread the signals
and was too generous with that guy, Ralph.

324
00:19:37,916 --> 00:19:40,500
Because I paid for the favor in advance.

325
00:19:40,583 --> 00:19:44,208
<i>When the time for our date came</i>
<i>and you didn't call, I called him again.</i>

326
00:19:44,291 --> 00:19:45,125
Blue Moon.

327
00:19:50,083 --> 00:19:51,708
Still, I called again.

328
00:19:53,250 --> 00:19:57,375
But I must confess I was irritated
and didn't conduct myself as a gentleman.

329
00:19:58,458 --> 00:20:01,666
<i>I never heard again from Ralph,</i>
<i>the 20,000 euros I sent him,</i>

330
00:20:01,750 --> 00:20:03,416
<i>or what became of the envelope.</i>

331
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:05,166
<i>Or, of course, from you.</i>

332
00:20:13,625 --> 00:20:15,916
We were both left waiting that same night.

333
00:20:18,041 --> 00:20:20,250
On different continents.

334
00:20:22,166 --> 00:20:23,750
But now we're here.

335
00:20:24,875 --> 00:20:25,875
Together.

336
00:20:27,291 --> 00:20:30,000
The fire of a volcano
couldn't keep us apart.

337
00:20:45,208 --> 00:20:47,916
Will you force me
to steal a kiss at gunpoint?

338
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:03,500
Point that at my head as much as you want,
but you won't get that kiss.

339
00:21:03,583 --> 00:21:05,500
Our moment's gone.

340
00:21:07,750 --> 00:21:09,708
I'm in love with someone else now.

341
00:21:10,583 --> 00:21:11,833
I know.

342
00:21:13,875 --> 00:21:14,875
I know.

343
00:21:14,958 --> 00:21:16,500
Yes, I saw her.

344
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:18,875
That Andalusian woman, so…

345
00:21:18,958 --> 00:21:20,750
So spirited. Yes.

346
00:21:22,833 --> 00:21:25,541
But there was someone
when you came into my life too.

347
00:21:25,625 --> 00:21:29,875
And still, I let you get in that taxi
and come with me to that concert.

348
00:21:30,375 --> 00:21:34,250
I gave you the chance
to completely change my world.

349
00:21:35,750 --> 00:21:38,000
And now you owe me the same chance.

350
00:21:38,875 --> 00:21:40,541
And that's why I came.

351
00:21:43,875 --> 00:21:45,208
One moment.

352
00:21:49,291 --> 00:21:51,125
Damián's so tense.

353
00:21:51,625 --> 00:21:52,916
Just like us.

354
00:21:53,875 --> 00:21:55,958
What do you mean?

355
00:21:56,458 --> 00:21:58,291
Did you notice what Berlin said?

356
00:21:58,375 --> 00:22:01,083
He's in a triangle
with the duke and the duchess.

357
00:22:01,875 --> 00:22:03,333
- Can I have your phone?
- Sure.

358
00:22:04,333 --> 00:22:05,666
Can you unlock it?

359
00:22:08,708 --> 00:22:09,916
I'm calling this guy.

360
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,708
What? Not over the phone.

361
00:22:12,791 --> 00:22:14,583
Let's solve this like adults.

362
00:22:15,250 --> 00:22:16,875
It's the 21st century, right?

363
00:22:17,708 --> 00:22:20,458
Do you still have him under Mom?

364
00:22:21,250 --> 00:22:22,250
Yes.

365
00:22:28,416 --> 00:22:29,625
<i>Hi, Keila.</i>

366
00:22:29,708 --> 00:22:32,166
No, it's Bruce, her boyfriend.

367
00:22:32,250 --> 00:22:34,583
I hope you didn't steal her phone
to snoop.

368
00:22:34,666 --> 00:22:36,875
No. Keila's here with me. Wait.

369
00:22:38,250 --> 00:22:41,166
- Say hi.
- Hi, Claudio.

370
00:22:41,250 --> 00:22:42,791
Is everything all right?

371
00:22:42,875 --> 00:22:45,291
<i>- Yes, everything's fine.</i>
<i>- It's all good, Claudio.</i>

372
00:22:45,375 --> 00:22:47,750
We wanted to invite you to dinner.

373
00:22:48,333 --> 00:22:49,833
- What do you think?
<i>- Hey.</i>

374
00:22:49,916 --> 00:22:53,041
You're not setting this up
to give me a beating, right?

375
00:22:53,125 --> 00:22:54,791
No, on the contrary.

376
00:22:54,875 --> 00:22:57,250
We can get margaritas at a Mexican place,

377
00:22:57,333 --> 00:22:58,833
talk about life, have fun…

378
00:22:58,916 --> 00:23:00,333
Go with the flow.

379
00:23:00,416 --> 00:23:03,458
We'll drop you a pin, okay?
Tomorrow or the day after. Bye.

380
00:23:03,541 --> 00:23:07,750
I swear I'm dying to know
how far you'll take this lover business…

381
00:23:42,208 --> 00:23:45,041
Genoveva. Sorry, I didn't recognize you.

382
00:23:46,375 --> 00:23:48,166
Blonde suits you.

383
00:23:48,958 --> 00:23:51,041
A wig, to throw Samuel off my scent.

384
00:23:51,125 --> 00:23:55,583
I went into Zara through a door,
put on the wig, and walked out another.

385
00:23:55,666 --> 00:23:57,208
Like a double agent.

386
00:23:58,041 --> 00:23:59,500
Quite the adrenaline rush.

387
00:24:00,875 --> 00:24:02,875
Come in, please.

388
00:24:12,166 --> 00:24:14,333
How Bohemian, living on a boat.

389
00:24:15,833 --> 00:24:16,666
Well…

390
00:24:21,125 --> 00:24:25,125
I was so pleased
to see you wear that white shirt.

391
00:24:27,416 --> 00:24:31,541
Well, actually, I had no choice.
You had an advantage.

392
00:24:32,125 --> 00:24:33,708
The dress code forced me.

393
00:24:35,416 --> 00:24:37,750
You're saying you were forced to be here?

394
00:24:37,833 --> 00:24:39,541
No.

395
00:24:41,083 --> 00:24:43,416
Here's what we agreed on for the date.

396
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:44,791
Your 500,000.

397
00:24:46,041 --> 00:24:47,666
In photocopies.

398
00:24:48,958 --> 00:24:51,000
You're more cunning than me.

399
00:24:51,583 --> 00:24:54,708
Although there's something
I still can't figure out.

400
00:24:54,791 --> 00:25:00,083
If you already have your money
and it seems I forced you to meet me here,

401
00:25:00,583 --> 00:25:02,583
why the nice dinner?

402
00:25:02,666 --> 00:25:05,041
With candles and flowers.

403
00:25:05,125 --> 00:25:06,333
What are you up to?

404
00:25:06,416 --> 00:25:07,416
Huh?

405
00:25:08,250 --> 00:25:09,416
What are you doing?

406
00:25:09,500 --> 00:25:13,041
Does it amuse you to see me
resort to disguises to see you?

407
00:25:13,125 --> 00:25:15,708
- No, it's not that.
- No? What is it, then?

408
00:25:15,791 --> 00:25:18,666
Why go this far?
Toying with a married woman excites you?

409
00:25:18,750 --> 00:25:21,583
No. I'm not toying. I'm in love with you.

410
00:25:21,666 --> 00:25:24,875
I avoid you by day,
fantasize with your portrait by night,

411
00:25:24,958 --> 00:25:27,458
keep your notes…

412
00:25:31,250 --> 00:25:34,750
I live in torment,
dreaming of you at all hours.

413
00:25:38,500 --> 00:25:41,166
I've dreamt of you too.

414
00:25:41,250 --> 00:25:44,250
We were on a boat like this.

415
00:25:45,666 --> 00:25:47,875
Do you want me to tell you
what happened now?

416
00:25:50,958 --> 00:25:52,583
Nothing will happen.

417
00:25:53,541 --> 00:25:54,833
Nothing will happen.

418
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:06,208
At first, I rejected you
because I am your husband's partner.

419
00:26:06,291 --> 00:26:08,583
Now I have two more reasons.

420
00:26:08,666 --> 00:26:10,875
The first one is…

421
00:26:10,958 --> 00:26:15,375
Well, the duke and I
are becoming friends and…

422
00:26:15,458 --> 00:26:17,458
He's a great guy. You know that.

423
00:26:17,541 --> 00:26:18,833
And the second?

424
00:26:20,250 --> 00:26:22,916
My marriage broke up over an infidelity.

425
00:26:24,875 --> 00:26:27,458
I know how devastating that is
to a person's life.

426
00:26:29,083 --> 00:26:30,125
I don't feel…

427
00:26:32,208 --> 00:26:33,833
capable of doing that to anyone.

428
00:26:34,833 --> 00:26:36,250
You speak…

429
00:26:37,708 --> 00:26:41,208
of infidelity, betrayal,

430
00:26:41,916 --> 00:26:43,708
of horrible things

431
00:26:44,291 --> 00:26:47,458
that cast a shadow
over this wonderful moment.

432
00:26:50,625 --> 00:26:51,791
I guess…

433
00:26:53,541 --> 00:26:55,541
I guess it's not a betrayal…

434
00:26:56,875 --> 00:26:58,458
for me to graze your hand.

435
00:27:01,166 --> 00:27:02,166
No.

436
00:27:02,250 --> 00:27:03,875
I guess not.

437
00:27:05,166 --> 00:27:06,833
And I guess

438
00:27:07,416 --> 00:27:09,416
it's not a sin

439
00:27:10,250 --> 00:27:12,208
to grab your arm.

440
00:27:13,458 --> 00:27:15,083
I guess not.

441
00:27:16,875 --> 00:27:18,125
And I think…

442
00:27:19,333 --> 00:27:20,791
I think it's also

443
00:27:21,916 --> 00:27:23,375
not a sin

444
00:27:24,375 --> 00:27:26,500
to breathe in your scent.

445
00:27:32,125 --> 00:27:33,666
I guess not.

446
00:27:36,833 --> 00:27:39,375
I think it's not a sin either

447
00:27:39,875 --> 00:27:42,375
for us to dance to your favorite song.

448
00:27:43,333 --> 00:27:44,708
I guess not.

449
00:28:49,250 --> 00:28:51,458
Let me propose something.

450
00:28:51,958 --> 00:28:54,583
Let's run away.

451
00:28:55,083 --> 00:28:56,416
Wait until it's all over,

452
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,708
and I promise you,
one minute after the theft,

453
00:28:59,791 --> 00:29:01,583
after I lose sight of your husband,

454
00:29:01,666 --> 00:29:04,166
we'll go to whichever continent you want,

455
00:29:05,250 --> 00:29:06,875
with the life you want

456
00:29:07,916 --> 00:29:09,583
and the soundtrack you want.

457
00:29:19,083 --> 00:29:21,125
Thank you for the proposal.

458
00:29:22,416 --> 00:29:24,291
I'll let you know my decision.

459
00:29:56,458 --> 00:29:58,125
What's this, Camille?

460
00:29:58,833 --> 00:30:00,083
A café au lait.

461
00:30:02,666 --> 00:30:04,125
Don't you remember?

462
00:30:04,625 --> 00:30:06,166
That's how it all started.

463
00:30:06,250 --> 00:30:08,250
At that bar counter.

464
00:30:09,666 --> 00:30:12,666
With the smell
of freshly-baked croissants.

465
00:30:34,458 --> 00:30:35,458
Paris…

466
00:30:36,333 --> 00:30:37,375
on your lips.

467
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:48,458
Are you starting to remember?

468
00:30:55,208 --> 00:30:58,125
You told me you had heard me sing a song

469
00:30:59,333 --> 00:31:00,375
<i>the night before.</i>

470
00:31:44,791 --> 00:31:48,208
I took off these panties for you
at that restaurant.

471
00:31:50,875 --> 00:31:52,041
They're yours.

472
00:32:03,958 --> 00:32:06,500
You can't possibly
not remember what we were.

473
00:32:07,250 --> 00:32:09,541
What we could do together.

474
00:32:10,125 --> 00:32:11,500
It's not over.

475
00:32:13,083 --> 00:32:16,416
I'm sure all we felt is here.

476
00:32:17,875 --> 00:32:19,791
Somewhere in your memory.

477
00:32:59,541 --> 00:33:01,875
I was just talking about you.

478
00:33:01,958 --> 00:33:03,541
Shut up, please.

479
00:33:06,125 --> 00:33:07,458
What's your name?

480
00:33:07,541 --> 00:33:10,083
Camille. Pleased to meet you.

481
00:33:10,666 --> 00:33:11,791
Candela.

482
00:33:13,375 --> 00:33:15,666
She's the woman
I fell in love with in Paris.

483
00:33:16,541 --> 00:33:18,125
Before I met you.

484
00:33:18,875 --> 00:33:21,708
If you were with her in Paris,
why get involved with me?

485
00:33:21,791 --> 00:33:24,791
Because I wasn't with her anymore.

486
00:33:25,916 --> 00:33:28,583
Not when I met you, nor two months before,

487
00:33:28,666 --> 00:33:33,250
when I couldn't meet her
at the Blue Moon in Singapore.

488
00:33:33,791 --> 00:33:35,583
I never heard from him again.

489
00:33:36,208 --> 00:33:40,416
Love stories end
when one of the two breaks up.

490
00:33:41,416 --> 00:33:43,708
In our case, neither of us did.

491
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:45,958
Camille.

492
00:33:46,958 --> 00:33:49,125
Ours was a relationship without phones,

493
00:33:49,208 --> 00:33:52,250
of waiting for the other
on different continents.

494
00:33:53,500 --> 00:33:54,666
It fell apart.

495
00:33:57,958 --> 00:33:59,375
I love you like crazy.

496
00:34:00,916 --> 00:34:02,291
Like never before.

497
00:34:04,375 --> 00:34:06,875
But we don't belong together.

498
00:34:08,541 --> 00:34:11,500
And it's not because of her.
I'm sure she's wonderful.

499
00:34:11,583 --> 00:34:15,083
We have different lives. I've never been
to the Blue Moon in Singapore.

500
00:34:15,166 --> 00:34:17,083
I don't even know what that is.

501
00:34:17,166 --> 00:34:18,500
But that's your thing.

502
00:34:18,583 --> 00:34:22,875
You're about what's shiny,
elegant, sophisticated.

503
00:34:24,375 --> 00:34:25,958
I'm more about, well…

504
00:34:26,666 --> 00:34:30,000
meeting my friends
at the fountain in the square,

505
00:34:30,083 --> 00:34:31,958
eating fried eggs with my hands,

506
00:34:32,916 --> 00:34:36,000
picking watermelons
with my father on Fridays…

507
00:34:36,083 --> 00:34:39,458
I'm more about the little things.
My life's full of little things.

508
00:34:39,541 --> 00:34:41,791
I love it,
because it's the only one I have.

509
00:34:43,041 --> 00:34:44,541
But you have 50,

510
00:34:44,625 --> 00:34:48,458
and in all 50, there's someone
who's chilling champagne for you.

511
00:34:54,875 --> 00:34:56,708
Of those 50 lives,

512
00:34:57,750 --> 00:34:59,500
I choose this one to be with you.

513
00:35:02,666 --> 00:35:04,833
This is the one that fascinates me.

514
00:35:06,833 --> 00:35:08,541
We do love each other, you and I.

515
00:35:12,250 --> 00:35:13,583
You know what?

516
00:35:14,541 --> 00:35:16,583
We would have loved each other forever.

517
00:35:18,541 --> 00:35:20,166
But it can't be.

518
00:35:22,416 --> 00:35:26,500
Because this has an expiration date,
and I'd rather set it myself today.

519
00:35:27,708 --> 00:35:32,125
And if you respect me even a little,
I ask you to please never look for me.

520
00:35:35,958 --> 00:35:36,958
Goodbye.

521
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:38,875
Goodbye, Camille.

522
00:37:06,208 --> 00:37:08,916
We'll take two pistols each
with two clips.

523
00:37:10,875 --> 00:37:12,416
Things can get complicated.

524
00:37:12,500 --> 00:37:13,833
We'll be on the street.

525
00:37:13,916 --> 00:37:16,750
There can be as few as eight.
Four in each vehicle.

526
00:37:17,333 --> 00:37:20,166
The fireproof suits will be in the back.

527
00:37:20,250 --> 00:37:22,375
The sooner you're out, the better. Got it?

528
00:37:22,458 --> 00:37:24,375
Yes. We'll go to the meeting point.

529
00:37:29,041 --> 00:37:31,416
When you have the suits, call me.

530
00:37:31,916 --> 00:37:33,291
I have things to do.

531
00:37:35,958 --> 00:37:36,958
I'll stay.

532
00:37:37,041 --> 00:37:39,875
I'll try to find out
how the armored igloo opens.

533
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:44,500
I want a clean operation.

534
00:37:44,583 --> 00:37:46,458
Fast. Leave no trace.

535
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:07,625
Now!

536
00:38:09,083 --> 00:38:10,291
We have a casualty.

537
00:38:15,250 --> 00:38:16,250
Pull.

538
00:39:51,166 --> 00:39:54,000
What are you doing here?
I told you not to look for me.

539
00:39:54,083 --> 00:39:56,041
Is this how you show me respect?

540
00:39:56,125 --> 00:39:57,541
No, not another step.

541
00:39:57,625 --> 00:39:59,333
She told you to leave.

542
00:40:04,750 --> 00:40:07,291
I'll leave as soon as you hear me out.

543
00:40:07,375 --> 00:40:08,208
I swear.

544
00:40:08,958 --> 00:40:10,958
Hey, let's go.

545
00:40:16,333 --> 00:40:17,791
Leave, you bastard.

546
00:40:18,291 --> 00:40:20,250
We saw you with the French woman.

547
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:29,750
Nothing happened with Camille.

548
00:40:31,708 --> 00:40:34,333
If anything, what happened
at that hotel is that you

549
00:40:34,833 --> 00:40:37,291
saw a way out to run away from me.

550
00:40:38,666 --> 00:40:40,958
- I don't want to talk to you.
- No, Candela.

551
00:40:41,041 --> 00:40:43,416
If you had thought I was being unfaithful,

552
00:40:43,500 --> 00:40:46,666
you would have set me alight yourself
with a can of gas.

553
00:40:51,625 --> 00:40:53,291
You got stage fright.

554
00:40:54,375 --> 00:40:58,208
And you hold on to a bottle of champagne
to say your life is not mine,

555
00:40:58,291 --> 00:41:00,333
but what you really fear is loving.

556
00:41:03,416 --> 00:41:04,666
Loving me so much.

557
00:41:09,458 --> 00:41:11,708
- But you know what?
- What?

558
00:41:11,791 --> 00:41:13,791
I have good news.

559
00:41:14,291 --> 00:41:15,625
I love you just as much.

560
00:41:17,250 --> 00:41:18,625
Or more. Look.

561
00:41:22,083 --> 00:41:23,916
It says so here, right?

562
00:41:25,250 --> 00:41:26,833
You told me yourself.

563
00:41:27,416 --> 00:41:32,208
But there's more here
that maybe you misread.

564
00:41:35,375 --> 00:41:37,208
You think I don't fit in your life.

565
00:41:38,083 --> 00:41:39,250
That's why…

566
00:41:39,333 --> 00:41:42,916
That's why I'll say it loud and clear
for all to hear.

567
00:41:46,500 --> 00:41:48,291
For me, it's an honor…

568
00:41:49,875 --> 00:41:53,000
An honor to come to this field
to pick watermelons

569
00:41:53,083 --> 00:41:54,833
or whatever with your family.

570
00:42:11,666 --> 00:42:14,958
Leave right now, or you leave in a box.

571
00:42:19,708 --> 00:42:21,083
I'll count to three.

572
00:42:23,583 --> 00:42:25,375
- One!
- Dad, please!

573
00:42:28,791 --> 00:42:29,833
Two!

574
00:42:37,916 --> 00:42:44,000
I won't leave, but I won't allow
this conversation to turn into a tragedy.

575
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:46,625
Simón, put the guns down.

576
00:42:53,875 --> 00:42:54,958
My name's not Simón.

577
00:42:59,250 --> 00:43:01,750
My name is Andrés de Fonollosa.

578
00:43:02,250 --> 00:43:05,208
I usually live
in Madrid's Las Letras district,

579
00:43:05,291 --> 00:43:07,416
above a café called Buenos Aires.

580
00:43:07,500 --> 00:43:10,500
I have a son called Rafael
from an early marriage,

581
00:43:10,583 --> 00:43:11,916
and I am a thief.

582
00:43:12,750 --> 00:43:17,208
And my only thought
since you left that hotel is

583
00:43:17,291 --> 00:43:21,458
how to prove to you
that I'm as madly in love as you.

584
00:43:22,750 --> 00:43:25,166
I’m heading straight toward you, my love.

585
00:43:27,041 --> 00:43:28,666
And that you can definitely

586
00:43:28,750 --> 00:43:30,500
have faith in me.

587
00:43:32,250 --> 00:43:37,041
And, well, the only way
I can think of doing it is…

588
00:43:38,750 --> 00:43:39,791
this.

589
00:43:41,500 --> 00:43:45,458
Could you look
into the pocket of my jacket, please?

590
00:44:02,833 --> 00:44:07,458
And this is the time
when you have to say yes

591
00:44:08,500 --> 00:44:09,500
or no.

592
00:44:17,291 --> 00:44:18,500
Yes, I do.

593
00:47:11,916 --> 00:47:16,625
Subtitle translation by:
Soledad Etchemendy

